{"name":"Blue Vortex","hex":"#3d4457","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"vortex","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A whirlwind, whirlpool, or similarly moving matter in the form of a spiral or column.","Anything that involves constant violent or chaotic activity around some centre."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mix using a vortex mixer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vortex"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vortex","blocks":[{"text":"In fluid dynamics, a vortex is a region in a fluid in which the flow revolves around an axis line, which may be straight or curved. Vortices form in stirred fluids and may be observed in smoke rings, whirlpools in the wake of a boat, and in the winds surrounding a tropical cyclone, tornado, or dust devil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg/330px-Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vortex"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Karman-Vortex.gif/500px-Karman-Vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Karman-Vortex.gif/500px-Karman-Vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Von Kármán vortex street behind a drinking straw; milk was poured into the water to make the current visible","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKarman-Vortex.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Whirpool_Clouds.jpg/500px-Whirpool_Clouds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Whirpool_Clouds.jpg/1280px-Whirpool_Clouds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Whirpool_Clouds.jpg/500px-Whirpool_Clouds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Kármán vortex street is shown in this photo, as winds from the west blow onto clouds that have formed over the mountains in the desert. This phenomenon observed from ground level is extremely rare, as most cloud-related Kármán vortex street activity is viewed from space.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhirpool_Clouds.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crow_instability_contrail.JPG/500px-Crow_instability_contrail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crow_instability_contrail.JPG/1280px-Crow_instability_contrail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crow_instability_contrail.JPG/500px-Crow_instability_contrail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Crow instability of a jet aeroplane's contrail visually demonstrates the vortex created in the atmosphere (gas fluid medium) by the passage of the aircraft.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrow_instability_contrail.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Rotational_vortex.gif/250px-Rotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Rotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Rotational_vortex.gif/250px-Rotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A rigid-body vortex","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARotational_vortex.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Irrotational_vortex.gif/250px-Irrotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Irrotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Irrotational_vortex.gif/250px-Irrotational_vortex.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An irrotational vortex","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIrrotational_vortex.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimed